<LessonPage page={page}>
<main class="wrap" data-core-duration="60–70 minutes" data-extension-duration="15–20 minutes">
  <p class="kicker"><span class="dot"></span> Vocabulary · Lesson 14</p>
  <h1>Media and news: <em>trace the claim before repeating it</em></h1>
  <p class="standfirst">A headline is a compressed invitation to read, not a complete argument and not proof. Responsible media English reconstructs the chain behind it: <span class="ex">Who published this?</span> <span class="ex">Who or what is the source?</span> <span class="ex">What exactly is claimed?</span> <span class="ex">What evidence supports it?</span> <span class="ex">What context changes its meaning?</span> <span class="ex">What is still uncertain?</span> The goal is not automatic trust or automatic distrust. It is accurate attribution and proportionate judgment.</p>
  <div class="meta-row">
    <span class="tag">60–70 min core</span>
    <span class="tag">format · claim · source · evidence</span>
    <span class="tag">context · attribution · verification</span>
    <span class="tag">15–20 min listening and retrieval</span>
  </div>

  <div class="lesson-flow" aria-label="Lesson flow">
    <div class="flow-step"><b>Notice</b><span>Compare a headline, report, source document, and reaction.</span></div>
    <div class="flow-step"><b>Separate</b><span>Identify format, claim, source, evidence, context, and viewpoint.</span></div>
    <div class="flow-step"><b>Verify</b><span>Trace numbers, quotations, images, dates, and independent coverage.</span></div>
    <div class="flow-step"><b>Use</b><span>Rewrite a misleading headline and deliver a careful briefing.</span></div>
  </div>

  <p class="note-line"><b>Can-do:</b> By the end, you can recognize a headline, news report, analysis, editorial, and press release; distinguish a publisher, author, quoted source, claim, evidence, context, and independent confirmation; separate a supported fact from opinion, inference, unverified information, and biased selection; use neutral and distancing attribution accurately; explain why an unnamed source is not the same as an unidentified online post; check dates, labels, numbers, quotations, images, and source independence; summarize a fictional report without increasing its certainty; and make key U.S. word stress and connected attribution phrases intelligible.</p>

  <h2>Two-minute retrieval</h2>
  <div class="lead-rule"></div>
  <div class="practice" data-choice-gap-drill data-success-message="Correct. You recovered reported speech for a later viewpoint, a past passive for the published report, and a countable expression with uncountable news.">
    <h3>Reactivate reporting, passive focus, and uncountable <span class="ex">news</span></h3>
    <div class="choice-bank" aria-label="Media foundation choices"><button class="choice-option" data-choice-option="would reopen">would reopen</button><button class="choice-option" data-choice-option="was published">was published</button><button class="choice-option" data-choice-option="a news report">a news report</button><button class="choice-option" data-choice-option="a news">a news</button></div>
    <div class="q"><span class="n">1</span><span>On Monday, the mayor said, “The road will reopen Friday.” Later that day, a reporter said the road <button class="choice-gap" data-choice-gap data-answer="would reopen" data-hint="The reporter is presenting the mayor’s future statement from a later past viewpoint." data-fix="The reporter said the road would reopen Friday.">choose</button> Friday.</span></div>
    <div class="q"><span class="n">2</span><span>Journalists published the report yesterday. With the report as the topic: “The report <button class="choice-gap" data-choice-gap data-answer="was published" data-hint="Use the past passive because the report received the publishing action." data-fix="The report was published yesterday.">choose</button> yesterday.”</span></div>
    <div class="q"><span class="n">3</span><span>One countable item appeared on the local website: “I read <button class="choice-gap" data-choice-gap data-answer="a news report" data-hint="News is uncountable; add a countable product noun after it." data-fix="I read a news report about the bridge.">choose</button> about the bridge.”</span></div>
    <div class="drill-actions"><button class="lesson-btn" data-check-choices>Check</button><button class="lesson-btn" data-reset-choices>Reset</button></div><p class="feedback" data-feedback></p>
  </div>

  <h2>Notice how one claim changes across four media moves</h2>
  <div class="lead-rule"></div>
  <div class="thinking-card">
    <h3>Fictional Metrovale bus-lane announcement</h3>
    <p><b>Headline:</b> <span class="ex">CITY BUS LANE “WILL END DOWNTOWN DELAYS”</span></p>
    <p><b>Source document:</b> The Metrovale Transportation Office press release says its simulation estimates that average travel time on two routes <span class="ex">could fall from 30 to 26 minutes</span> if the lane is enforced consistently.</p>
    <p><b>News report:</b> The pilot begins Monday and will run for eight weeks. Actual travel-time results are not available yet. The reporter asked the office to publish the model assumptions and said measured results should be compared with the estimate.</p>
    <p><b>Reader comment:</b> <span class="ex">“This is obviously the best transportation plan the city has ever created.”</span></p>
    <ul>
      <li>Where does the absolute promise appear?</li>
      <li>What evidence exists now, and what evidence does not exist yet?</li>
      <li>Which organization produced the source document?</li>
      <li>Which line expresses a personal judgment?</li>
    </ul>
  </div>
  <div class="practice" data-choice-gap-drill data-success-message="Correct. You separated the headline’s promise, the transportation office as source-document publisher, the model estimate, and the reader’s opinion.">
    <h3>Discovery check: identify the job of each line</h3>
    <div class="choice-bank" aria-label="Metrovale media move choices"><button class="choice-option" data-choice-option="will end downtown delays">will end downtown delays</button><button class="choice-option" data-choice-option="the Metrovale Transportation Office">the Metrovale Transportation Office</button><button class="choice-option" data-choice-option="a model estimate">a model estimate</button><button class="choice-option" data-choice-option="the best plan the city has ever created">the best plan the city has ever created</button></div>
    <div class="q"><span class="n">1</span><span>The headline’s absolute promise is <button class="choice-gap" data-choice-gap data-answer="will end downtown delays" data-hint="Find the wording that presents the future result as certain." data-fix="The headline promises that the lane will end downtown delays.">choose</button>.</span></div>
    <div class="q"><span class="n">2</span><span>The organization that published the source document is <button class="choice-gap" data-choice-gap data-answer="the Metrovale Transportation Office" data-hint="Find the named office behind the press release." data-fix="The Metrovale Transportation Office published the press release.">choose</button>.</span></div>
    <div class="q"><span class="n">3</span><span>The evidence available before the pilot is <button class="choice-gap" data-choice-gap data-answer="a model estimate" data-hint="Actual travel times have not been measured yet." data-fix="Before the pilot, the available evidence is a model estimate.">choose</button>, not a measured result.</span></div>
    <div class="q"><span class="n">4</span><span>The personal judgment is that this is <button class="choice-gap" data-choice-gap data-answer="the best plan the city has ever created" data-hint="Choose the evaluative superlative in the reader comment." data-fix="The reader calls it the best plan the city has ever created.">choose</button>.</span></div>
    <div class="drill-actions"><button class="lesson-btn" data-check-choices>Check</button><button class="lesson-btn" data-reset-choices>Reset</button></div><p class="feedback" data-feedback></p>
  </div>

  <h2>Identify the product before evaluating its purpose</h2>
  <div class="lead-rule"></div>
  <table>
    <thead><tr><th>format</th><th>usual job</th><th>important limit</th></tr></thead>
    <tbody>
      <tr><td><span class="ex">headline</span></td><td>name or frame the story briefly</td><td>compressed wording cannot carry every condition or detail</td></tr>
      <tr><td><span class="ex">news report</span></td><td>report attributed, checked information about events</td><td>quality depends on sourcing, evidence, corrections, and context</td></tr>
      <tr><td><span class="ex">analysis</span></td><td>explain background, patterns, meaning, or likely consequences</td><td>includes interpretation and should be labeled clearly</td></tr>
      <tr><td><span class="ex">editorial or opinion piece</span></td><td>argue for a judgment, position, or recommendation</td><td>may use facts, but its main purpose is not neutral event reporting</td></tr>
      <tr><td><span class="ex">press release</span></td><td>present an organization’s announcement in its own voice</td><td>primary evidence for what the organization announced, not independent proof that every claim is correct</td></tr>
    </tbody>
  </table>
  <p><span class="ex">Sponsored content</span> or <span class="ex">an advertisement</span> is paid promotional material and should be labeled. A label helps the reader understand purpose, but it does not settle truth by itself. A press release can contain accurate measurements; an analysis can make a weak inference; an opinion piece can cite strong facts; and a report can still omit relevant context. Evaluate the actual claim and support.</p>
  <div class="practice" data-choice-gap-drill data-success-message="Correct. You classified the short title, event report, interpretive explanation, argued viewpoint, and organization-produced announcement by purpose.">
    <h3>Choose the format from its stated purpose</h3>
    <div class="choice-bank" aria-label="Media format choices"><button class="choice-option" data-choice-option="headline">headline</button><button class="choice-option" data-choice-option="news report">news report</button><button class="choice-option" data-choice-option="analysis">analysis</button><button class="choice-option" data-choice-option="editorial">editorial</button><button class="choice-option" data-choice-option="press release">press release</button></div>
    <div class="q"><span class="n">1</span><span>The seven-word title appears above the complete story and compresses its main angle. It is the <button class="choice-gap" data-choice-gap data-answer="headline" data-hint="Choose the brief title above the full article." data-fix="The brief title is the headline.">choose</button>.</span></div>
    <div class="q"><span class="n">2</span><span>A journalist attributes statements, checks the meeting record, and describes what happened Tuesday. The product is a <button class="choice-gap" data-choice-gap data-answer="news report" data-hint="Choose the format whose main job is attributed event reporting." data-fix="The journalist produced a news report about Tuesday’s meeting.">choose</button>.</span></div>
    <div class="q"><span class="n">3</span><span>A labeled article explains how three years of housing data connect to the new policy and where uncertainty remains. It is <button class="choice-gap" data-choice-gap data-answer="analysis" data-hint="The main job is interpretation of background and patterns." data-fix="The labeled article is analysis.">choose</button>.</span></div>
    <div class="q"><span class="n">4</span><span>The newspaper’s board argues that the city should approve the proposal. The labeled argument is an <button class="choice-gap" data-choice-gap data-answer="editorial" data-hint="Choose the format that presents the publication’s argued position." data-fix="The newspaper board published an editorial supporting the proposal.">choose</button>.</span></div>
    <div class="q"><span class="n">5</span><span>A company’s communications office announces its own new product in a document sent to journalists. The document is a <button class="choice-gap" data-choice-gap data-answer="press release" data-hint="Choose the organization-produced announcement sent to the media." data-fix="The company issued a press release about its product.">choose</button>.</span></div>
    <div class="drill-actions"><button class="lesson-btn" data-check-choices>Check</button><button class="lesson-btn" data-reset-choices>Reset</button></div><p class="feedback" data-feedback></p>
  </div>

  <h2>Map the chain: publisher, source, claim, evidence, context, confirmation</h2>
  <div class="lead-rule"></div>
  <p>The <b>publisher</b> makes the item public; the <b>author or reporter</b> created the item; a <b>source</b> supplies information or gives access to a document, observation, or dataset. A <b>claim</b> is a statement presented as true. <b>Evidence</b> is information used to support, weaken, or test that claim. <b>Context</b> includes surrounding facts that affect interpretation. <b>Independent confirmation</b> comes from a genuinely separate route to the information, not three sites copying the same release.</p>
  <p><span class="ex">Primary source</span> means close to the original event, document, measurement, or statement. It does not mean automatically neutral or correct. A city memo is primary evidence for what the city recorded or announced, but another qualified source may be needed to evaluate its method or conclusion. Always ask what exact question the source can answer.</p>
  <div class="practice" data-choice-gap-drill data-success-message="Correct. You distinguished the statement being tested, its direct support, interpretation-changing context, the origin of information, and truly separate confirmation.">
    <h3>Name the role each piece plays in the verification chain</h3>
    <div class="choice-bank" aria-label="Claim evidence context source confirmation choices"><button class="choice-option" data-choice-option="claim">claim</button><button class="choice-option" data-choice-option="evidence">evidence</button><button class="choice-option" data-choice-option="context">context</button><button class="choice-option" data-choice-option="source">source</button><button class="choice-option" data-choice-option="independent confirmation">independent confirmation</button></div>
    <div class="q"><span class="n">1</span><span>The sentence “Average travel time fell by four minutes” is the statement being tested. It is the <button class="choice-gap" data-choice-gap data-answer="claim" data-hint="Choose the term for a statement presented as true." data-fix="The statement about travel time is the claim.">choose</button>.</span></div>
    <div class="q"><span class="n">2</span><span>Timestamped travel-time records from every pilot bus are offered to test the four-minute statement. The records are <button class="choice-gap" data-choice-gap data-answer="evidence" data-hint="Choose the information used to support or test the claim." data-fix="The timestamped bus records are evidence for the travel-time claim.">choose</button>.</span></div>
    <div class="q"><span class="n">3</span><span>Road construction closed one parallel street during half the pilot. That surrounding fact changes how the travel-time result should be interpreted, so it is important <button class="choice-gap" data-choice-gap data-answer="context" data-hint="Choose the surrounding information that affects interpretation." data-fix="The parallel road closure is important context.">choose</button>.</span></div>
    <div class="q"><span class="n">4</span><span>The transit analyst who supplied the timestamped records is the reporter’s named <button class="choice-gap" data-choice-gap data-answer="source" data-hint="Choose the person who supplied the information or access." data-fix="The transit analyst is the reporter’s named source for the records.">choose</button>.</span></div>
    <div class="q"><span class="n">5</span><span>A university team separately collected its own travel-time sample and found a similar pattern. Its separate data provide <button class="choice-gap" data-choice-gap data-answer="independent confirmation" data-hint="The team did not copy the city release; it used a separate route and dataset." data-fix="The university team’s separate sample provides independent confirmation.">choose</button>.</span></div>
    <div class="drill-actions"><button class="lesson-btn" data-check-choices>Check</button><button class="lesson-btn" data-reset-choices>Reset</button></div><p class="feedback" data-feedback></p>
  </div>

  <h2>Distinguish evidence status from viewpoint</h2>
  <div class="lead-rule"></div>
  <table>
    <thead><tr><th>category</th><th>decision question</th><th>language clue</th></tr></thead>
    <tbody>
      <tr><td><span class="ex">supported fact</span></td><td>Has adequate reliable evidence established the statement?</td><td><span class="ex">Records show the vote occurred Tuesday.</span></td></tr>
      <tr><td><span class="ex">opinion</span></td><td>Does the statement express a judgment, value, or preference?</td><td><span class="ex">The debate was disappointing.</span></td></tr>
      <tr><td><span class="ex">inference</span></td><td>Is the speaker drawing a conclusion from evidence?</td><td><span class="ex">The pattern may indicate growing demand.</span></td></tr>
      <tr><td><span class="ex">unverified claim</span></td><td>Has the statement received enough checking yet?</td><td><span class="ex">The post alleges a closure, but no notice confirms it.</span></td></tr>
      <tr><td><span class="ex">bias</span></td><td>Does repeated selection, emphasis, or language favor a viewpoint?</td><td><span class="ex">The segment quotes only supporters and labels critics emotionally.</span></td></tr>
    </tbody>
  </table>
  <p>A <b>factual claim</b> is a statement that evidence could establish or reject. Calling it checkable does not mean it has already been verified. <span class="ex">Unverified</span> means the available checking is insufficient, not automatically false. <span class="ex">Bias</span> can influence selection, framing, and interpretation, but it does not make every detail false. A neutral tone also does not prove accuracy. Check each important claim and the evidence behind it.</p>
  <div class="practice" data-choice-gap-drill data-success-message="Correct. You separated an established record, a judgment, a cautious conclusion, an unchecked post, and repeated one-sided framing.">
    <h3>Classify the statement from the supplied evidence status</h3>
    <div class="choice-bank" aria-label="Evidence status choices"><button class="choice-option" data-choice-option="supported fact">supported fact</button><button class="choice-option" data-choice-option="opinion">opinion</button><button class="choice-option" data-choice-option="inference">inference</button><button class="choice-option" data-choice-option="unverified claim">unverified claim</button><button class="choice-option" data-choice-option="bias">bias</button></div>
    <div class="q"><span class="n">1</span><span>The certified meeting record and complete video both show that the council voted Tuesday. In this context, “The vote occurred Tuesday” is a <button class="choice-gap" data-choice-gap data-answer="supported fact" data-hint="Two adequate records establish the event and date in this exercise." data-fix="The vote occurred Tuesday is presented as a supported fact.">choose</button>.</span></div>
    <div class="q"><span class="n">2</span><span>A columnist writes, “The new station is ugly.” The adjective expresses aesthetic judgment, so the statement is an <button class="choice-gap" data-choice-gap data-answer="opinion" data-hint="Ugly expresses a personal evaluation rather than a measured property here." data-fix="The statement that the station is ugly is an opinion.">choose</button>.</span></div>
    <div class="q"><span class="n">3</span><span>Sales rose in a small early sample. The analyst says this <span class="ex">may indicate</span> stronger demand. The cautious conclusion is an <button class="choice-gap" data-choice-gap data-answer="inference" data-hint="The analyst is interpreting a pattern rather than reporting only the raw number." data-fix="The possible stronger demand is an inference from the early sales data.">choose</button>.</span></div>
    <div class="q"><span class="n">4</span><span>An unidentified account posts that the airport is closed, but no airport notice or credible report confirms it. The closure is currently an <button class="choice-gap" data-choice-gap data-answer="unverified claim" data-hint="The available checking is insufficient; that does not yet establish true or false." data-fix="The airport-closure statement is an unverified claim.">choose</button>.</span></div>
    <div class="q"><span class="n">5</span><span>Across twelve segments, a channel quotes only one side and consistently uses insulting labels for the other side. This repeated selection and framing is evidence of <button class="choice-gap" data-choice-gap data-answer="bias" data-hint="Choose the term for a sustained pattern favoring one viewpoint." data-fix="The sustained one-sided selection and emotional labeling indicate bias.">choose</button>.</span></div>
    <div class="drill-actions"><button class="lesson-btn" data-check-choices>Check</button><button class="lesson-btn" data-reset-choices>Reset</button></div><p class="feedback" data-feedback></p>
  </div>

  <h2>Attribute the message without inventing certainty or stance</h2>
  <div class="lead-rule"></div>
  <p>Use one complete frame: <span class="ex">According to the transit office, the pilot begins Monday</span> or <span class="ex">The transit office says the pilot begins Monday</span>. Do not combine them as <span class="ex">According to the office says</span>. Use <span class="ex">said + clause</span> and <span class="ex">told + person + clause</span>. A direct quotation presents exact supplied words in quotation marks; a paraphrase reports meaning without pretending to reproduce every word.</p>
  <p><span class="ex">Says, said</span>, and <span class="ex">reports</span> are relatively neutral. <span class="ex">Claims</span> can create distance from the statement; <span class="ex">admits</span> adds the writer’s judgment that the information is reluctantly accepted or unfavorable. Do not change a neutral source verb into a loaded verb without a reason.</p>
  <p>An <b>unnamed or anonymous source</b> is not necessarily an unknown online account. In professional reporting, the journalist and editor may know the person’s identity while withholding it from the public for a stated reason. Because readers cannot assess the person directly, the report should explain the source’s relevant access when safe, seek corroboration, and limit what the source alone can establish. An unidentified post with no accountable reporting chain is a different situation.</p>
  <div class="practice" data-choice-gap-drill data-success-message="Correct. You used one attribution frame, supplied a listener after told, preserved exact quotation, kept neutral attribution, and distinguished a protected source from an unidentified post.">
    <h3>Choose the attribution relationship the context requires</h3>
    <div class="choice-bank" aria-label="Attribution choices"><button class="choice-option" data-choice-option="According to the report">According to the report</button><button class="choice-option" data-choice-option="told the reporter">told the reporter</button><button class="choice-option" data-choice-option="direct quotation">direct quotation</button><button class="choice-option" data-choice-option="said">said</button><button class="choice-option" data-choice-option="unnamed source">unnamed source</button></div>
    <div class="q"><span class="n">1</span><span>Use one source-noun frame before the comma: “<button class="choice-gap" data-choice-gap data-answer="According to the report" data-hint="Choose according to plus the source noun, without adding says." data-fix="According to the report, costs fell in May.">choose</button>, costs fell in May.”</span></div>
    <div class="q"><span class="n">2</span><span>The analyst addressed a specific listener: “The analyst <button class="choice-gap" data-choice-gap data-answer="told the reporter" data-hint="Told requires the person who received the message." data-fix="The analyst told the reporter that the sample was small.">choose</button> that the sample was small.”</span></div>
    <div class="q"><span class="n">3</span><span>The article reproduces the mayor’s supplied exact words inside quotation marks. That presentation is a <button class="choice-gap" data-choice-gap data-answer="direct quotation" data-hint="Quotation marks present the wording as exact, not merely paraphrased." data-fix="The mayor’s exact words appear as a direct quotation.">choose</button>.</span></div>
    <div class="q"><span class="n">4</span><span>The transcript only records that the official gave the information. The neutral report should say the official <button class="choice-gap" data-choice-gap data-answer="said" data-hint="Do not add reluctance, doubt, or another stance that the transcript does not show." data-fix="The neutral report says the official said the bridge was open.">choose</button> the bridge was open.</span></div>
    <div class="q"><span class="n">5</span><span>The journalist and editor know the inspector’s identity, explain that the inspector reviewed the records, and protect the name because of a documented retaliation risk. To the public, the inspector is an <button class="choice-gap" data-choice-gap data-answer="unnamed source" data-hint="The reporting team knows the identity, but the name is withheld from readers." data-fix="The inspector is an unnamed source whose relevant access is explained.">choose</button>.</span></div>
    <div class="drill-actions"><button class="lesson-btn" data-check-choices>Check</button><button class="lesson-btn" data-reset-choices>Reset</button></div><p class="feedback" data-feedback></p>
  </div>

  <h2>Trace the item before sharing it</h2>
  <div class="lead-rule"></div>
  <ol>
    <li><b>Pause.</b> A strong emotional reaction is a reason to slow down, not proof that the item is true or false.</li>
    <li><b>Open the full item.</b> Check the author, publisher, date, update or correction note, content label, and whether the headline matches the body.</li>
    <li><b>Trace the central claim.</b> Follow the quotation, statistic, image, video, document, or dataset to the earliest available responsible source.</li>
    <li><b>Inspect context and method.</b> Ask about the sample, baseline, denominator, timeframe, comparison, model, measurement, missing group, image date, location, and caption.</li>
    <li><b>Compare genuinely independent coverage.</b> Several pages repeating one wire report or press release are not several independent confirmations.</li>
  </ol>
  <p>Numbers need grammar and context. A change from 50% to 60% is an increase of <b>10 percentage points</b>, or a <b>20% relative increase</b> compared with the original 50%. A headline saying <span class="ex">up 20%</span> should state what changed, from what baseline, among whom, and during what period. A modeled estimate is not a measured outcome, one organization does not represent every organization, and an association does not by itself prove causation.</p>
  <p>An old image can be authentic but misleading when attached to a new event. Check the earliest available appearance, original caption, date, location, crop, and whether the image shows what the current claim says it shows. If uncertainty remains, say so or do not share the claim.</p>
  <div class="practice" data-choice-gap-drill data-success-message="Correct. You selected the full report, original evidence, denominator and baseline, genuinely independent coverage, and original image context as the checks that answer each specific gap.">
    <h3>Choose the next check from what is missing</h3>
    <div class="choice-bank" aria-label="Verification action choices"><button class="choice-option" data-choice-option="open the full report and check its date">open the full report and check its date</button><button class="choice-option" data-choice-option="find the original dataset">find the original dataset</button><button class="choice-option" data-choice-option="identify the baseline and denominator">identify the baseline and denominator</button><button class="choice-option" data-choice-option="compare genuinely independent coverage">compare genuinely independent coverage</button><button class="choice-option" data-choice-option="find the image’s original context">find the image’s original context</button></div>
    <div class="q"><span class="n">1</span><span>A screenshot shows only a dramatic headline, with no article body or visible date. First, <button class="choice-gap" data-choice-gap data-answer="open the full report and check its date" data-hint="Recover the complete item and its time context before judging the screenshot." data-fix="Open the full report and check its date, author, publisher, label, and body.">choose</button>.</span></div>
    <div class="q"><span class="n">2</span><span>An article says “the data prove it” but links only to another summary. To inspect the actual measurements, <button class="choice-gap" data-choice-gap data-answer="find the original dataset" data-hint="Move from summaries toward the underlying measurements and method." data-fix="Find the original dataset and its method notes.">choose</button>.</span></div>
    <div class="q"><span class="n">3</span><span>A post says “complaints rose 200%” but gives no starting number or group size. To interpret the percentage, <button class="choice-gap" data-choice-gap data-answer="identify the baseline and denominator" data-hint="A relative increase needs the original number and the population being counted." data-fix="Identify the baseline and denominator behind the 200% claim.">choose</button>.</span></div>
    <div class="q"><span class="n">4</span><span>Five websites repeat identical wording from one company release. To seek a separate route to the facts, <button class="choice-gap" data-choice-gap data-answer="compare genuinely independent coverage" data-hint="Repeated wording can be one source copied five times." data-fix="Compare coverage based on genuinely independent reporting or evidence.">choose</button>.</span></div>
    <div class="q"><span class="n">5</span><span>A striking flood photo has no photographer, date, location, or original caption. To test whether it belongs to the current event, <button class="choice-gap" data-choice-gap data-answer="find the image’s original context" data-hint="Trace the image before assuming the current caption is accurate." data-fix="Find the image’s original context, including date, location, caption, and crop.">choose</button>.</span></div>
    <div class="drill-actions"><button class="lesson-btn" data-check-choices>Check</button><button class="lesson-btn" data-reset-choices>Reset</button></div><p class="feedback" data-feedback></p>
  </div>

  <h2>Sound natural: Make attribution and media vocabulary audible</h2>
  <div class="lead-rule"></div>
  <p><span class="ex">News</span> is one syllable, /nuz/, and is uncountable: <span class="ex">some news, a piece of news, a news report</span>. Keep the stressed syllable clear in <span class="ex">HEAD-line</span> /ˈhɛdˌlaɪn/, <span class="ex">re-PORT</span> /rɪˈpɔrt/, and <span class="ex">re-LI-a-ble</span> /rɪˈlaɪəbəl/. <span class="ex">Source</span> is one syllable, /sɔrs/. The noun <span class="ex">RE-cord</span> /ˈrɛkərd/ and verb <span class="ex">re-CORD</span> /rɪˈkɔrd/ shift stress. In common U.S. English, <span class="ex">data</span> is often /ˈdeɪtə/; /ˈdætə/ is also established.</p>
  <p>Link function words lightly in <span class="ex">according_to the report</span> /əˈkɔrdɪŋ tə ðə rɪˈpɔrt/. In a careful correction, stress the contrast: <span class="ex">The model <b>ESTIMATED</b> a change; the pilot has not <b>MEASURED</b> it yet.</span> Clarity and accurate attribution matter more than copying one accent.</p>
  <div class="quiz" data-quiz data-success-message="Correct. You heard the certainty contrast, source relationship, uncountable news, record stress shift, and modeled-versus-measured evidence distinction.">
    <h3>Tutor-read listening: recover the source and evidence status</h3>
    <p class="note-line"><b>Tutor:</b> Read each line naturally once, then repeat if requested. <b>Learner:</b> Choose the relationship or sound pattern you heard.</p>
    <div class="quiz-list">
      <div class="quiz-item" data-quiz-item data-answer="The headline promises certainty, but the report presents a possibility." data-hint="Listen for will in the headline and could in the attributed report." data-fix="The headline promises certainty, but the report presents a possibility."><p class="quiz-question">1. Tutor line: “The headline says the lane will end delays, but the report says travel times could fall.” What contrast did you hear?</p><div class="quiz-options"><button class="quiz-option" data-quiz-option="The headline promises certainty, but the report presents a possibility.">The headline promises certainty, but the report presents a possibility.</button><button class="quiz-option" data-quiz-option="Both lines report an already measured result with equal certainty.">Both lines report an already measured result with equal certainty.</button><button class="quiz-option" data-quiz-option="The report proves the headline is false before the pilot begins.">The report proves the headline is false before the pilot begins.</button></div></div>
      <div class="quiz-item" data-quiz-item data-answer="The transportation office is the attributed source of the estimate." data-hint="According to introduces the source noun." data-fix="The transportation office is the attributed source of the estimate."><p class="quiz-question">2. Tutor line: “According to the transportation office, the model estimates a shorter trip.” What is the office’s role in this sentence?</p><div class="quiz-options"><button class="quiz-option" data-quiz-option="The transportation office is the attributed source of the estimate.">The transportation office is the attributed source of the estimate.</button><button class="quiz-option" data-quiz-option="The transportation office is the listener after told.">The transportation office is the listener after told.</button><button class="quiz-option" data-quiz-option="The transportation office is an adjective describing the trip.">The transportation office is an adjective describing the trip.</button></div></div>
      <div class="quiz-item" data-quiz-item data-answer="News is uncountable, so the speaker says a piece of news." data-hint="Listen for a countable piece before the one-syllable noun news." data-fix="News is uncountable, so the speaker says a piece of news."><p class="quiz-question">3. Tutor line: “I heard a surprising piece of news.” Why is <span class="ex">piece</span> present?</p><div class="quiz-options"><button class="quiz-option" data-quiz-option="News is uncountable, so the speaker says a piece of news.">News is uncountable, so the speaker says a piece of news.</button><button class="quiz-option" data-quiz-option="News is a plural count noun that always needs one pieces.">News is a plural count noun that always needs one pieces.</button><button class="quiz-option" data-quiz-option="Piece changes news into a verb.">Piece changes news into a verb.</button></div></div>
      <div class="quiz-item" data-quiz-item data-answer="The noun has first-syllable stress; the verb has second-syllable stress." data-hint="Listen to RE-cord for the stored document and re-CORD for the action." data-fix="The noun has first-syllable stress; the verb has second-syllable stress."><p class="quiz-question">4. Tutor line: “The RE-cord is public, and the station will re-CORD the meeting.” Which stress pattern did you hear?</p><div class="quiz-options"><button class="quiz-option" data-quiz-option="The noun has first-syllable stress; the verb has second-syllable stress.">The noun has first-syllable stress; the verb has second-syllable stress.</button><button class="quiz-option" data-quiz-option="Both uses must have first-syllable stress.">Both uses must have first-syllable stress.</button><button class="quiz-option" data-quiz-option="Both uses are one-syllable words.">Both uses are one-syllable words.</button></div></div>
      <div class="quiz-item" data-quiz-item data-answer="The model produced an estimate; the pilot has not produced actual results yet." data-hint="The stressed contrast is estimated versus measured." data-fix="The model produced an estimate; the pilot has not produced actual results yet."><p class="quiz-question">5. Tutor line: “The model ESTIMATED a change; the pilot has not MEASURED it yet.” What evidence distinction is emphasized?</p><div class="quiz-options"><button class="quiz-option" data-quiz-option="The model produced an estimate; the pilot has not produced actual results yet.">The model produced an estimate; the pilot has not produced actual results yet.</button><button class="quiz-option" data-quiz-option="The pilot measured every result before it began.">The pilot measured every result before it began.</button><button class="quiz-option" data-quiz-option="A model and a measured outcome are always identical.">A model and a measured outcome are always identical.</button></div></div>
    </div>
    <div class="drill-actions"><button class="lesson-btn" data-check-quiz>Check</button><button class="lesson-btn" data-reset-quiz>Reset</button></div><p class="feedback" data-feedback></p>
  </div>

  <h2>Verify a dramatic percentage without deleting the real result</h2>
  <div class="lead-rule"></div>
  <div class="thinking-card">
    <h3>Fictional Clearwater cafeteria pilot file</h3>
    <p><b>Viral headline:</b> <span class="ex">CITY CUTS FOOD WASTE BY 80% IN ONE MONTH</span></p>
    <p><b>Original pilot memo:</b> At one of Clearwater’s 24 public cafeterias, discarded unopened packaged food fell from 50 pounds to 10 pounds after a donation system began. That category fell by 80%. Total food waste at the same cafeteria, including preparation scraps and plate waste, fell from 500 pounds to 455 pounds, a 9% decrease.</p>
    <p><b>Scope:</b> The pilot lasted one month at one cafeteria. No citywide measurement has been completed. The city sanitation office produced the memo and published its measurement guide.</p>
    <p><b>Independent comment:</b> A university waste researcher who was not involved in the pilot called the result promising but said the city should use the same method at more cafeterias for several months before making a citywide claim.</p>
  </div>
  <div class="quiz" data-quiz data-success-message="Correct. You preserved the 80% category result, the 9% total-site result, the one-site scope, the memo’s source status, and the next evidence needed for a citywide conclusion.">
    <h3>Verification check: what does the fictional file actually support?</h3>
    <div class="quiz-list">
      <div class="quiz-item" data-quiz-item data-answer="Discarded unopened packaged food at one cafeteria fell from 50 pounds to 10 pounds." data-hint="The 80% figure belongs to one named category at one site." data-fix="Discarded unopened packaged food at one cafeteria fell from 50 pounds to 10 pounds."><p class="quiz-question">1. What exactly decreased by 80%?</p><div class="quiz-options"><button class="quiz-option" data-quiz-option="Discarded unopened packaged food at one cafeteria fell from 50 pounds to 10 pounds.">Discarded unopened packaged food at one cafeteria fell from 50 pounds to 10 pounds.</button><button class="quiz-option" data-quiz-option="All food waste at all 24 cafeterias fell by 80% for a full year.">All food waste at all 24 cafeterias fell by 80% for a full year.</button><button class="quiz-option" data-quiz-option="Preparation scraps increased from 10 pounds to 50 pounds citywide.">Preparation scraps increased from 10 pounds to 50 pounds citywide.</button></div></div>
      <div class="quiz-item" data-quiz-item data-answer="Total food waste at the pilot cafeteria fell by 9%, from 500 pounds to 455 pounds." data-hint="Use the total-site numbers, not the smaller packaged-food category." data-fix="Total food waste at the pilot cafeteria fell by 9%, from 500 pounds to 455 pounds."><p class="quiz-question">2. What happened to total food waste at the pilot cafeteria?</p><div class="quiz-options"><button class="quiz-option" data-quiz-option="Total food waste at the pilot cafeteria fell by 9%, from 500 pounds to 455 pounds.">Total food waste at the pilot cafeteria fell by 9%, from 500 pounds to 455 pounds.</button><button class="quiz-option" data-quiz-option="Total food waste fell by 80% at every cafeteria.">Total food waste fell by 80% at every cafeteria.</button><button class="quiz-option" data-quiz-option="The file contains no total food-waste measurement.">The file contains no total food-waste measurement.</button></div></div>
      <div class="quiz-item" data-quiz-item data-answer="It turns one category at one cafeteria into a claim about all city food waste." data-hint="Compare the headline’s citywide scope with the memo’s site and category." data-fix="The headline turns one category at one cafeteria into a claim about all city food waste."><p class="quiz-question">3. Why is the viral headline misleading?</p><div class="quiz-options"><button class="quiz-option" data-quiz-option="It turns one category at one cafeteria into a claim about all city food waste.">It turns one category at one cafeteria into a claim about all city food waste.</button><button class="quiz-option" data-quiz-option="It says the pilot lasted one month, which is the memo’s exact timeframe.">It says the pilot lasted one month, which is the memo’s exact timeframe.</button><button class="quiz-option" data-quiz-option="It reports that food exists in cafeterias.">It reports that food exists in cafeterias.</button></div></div>
      <div class="quiz-item" data-quiz-item data-answer="The city memo is primary evidence for its recorded pilot measurements, but it is not independent confirmation of a citywide effect." data-hint="Keep primary source status separate from independent confirmation and broader scope." data-fix="The city memo is primary evidence for its recorded pilot measurements, but it is not independent confirmation of a citywide effect."><p class="quiz-question">4. How should the city memo be described?</p><div class="quiz-options"><button class="quiz-option" data-quiz-option="The city memo is primary evidence for its recorded pilot measurements, but it is not independent confirmation of a citywide effect.">The city memo is primary evidence for its recorded pilot measurements, but it is not independent confirmation of a citywide effect.</button><button class="quiz-option" data-quiz-option="Because the city wrote it, every number is automatically false.">Because the city wrote it, every number is automatically false.</button><button class="quiz-option" data-quiz-option="Because it is primary, it automatically proves every future city result.">Because it is primary, it automatically proves every future city result.</button></div></div>
      <div class="quiz-item" data-quiz-item data-answer="Measure several cafeterias for several months with the same published method." data-hint="A citywide conclusion needs broader sites, longer time, and comparable measurement." data-fix="Measure several cafeterias for several months with the same published method."><p class="quiz-question">5. What next step would strengthen a broader conclusion?</p><div class="quiz-options"><button class="quiz-option" data-quiz-option="Measure several cafeterias for several months with the same published method.">Measure several cafeterias for several months with the same published method.</button><button class="quiz-option" data-quiz-option="Repeat the dramatic headline more often without collecting more data.">Repeat the dramatic headline more often without collecting more data.</button><button class="quiz-option" data-quiz-option="Remove the 9% result so readers see only the larger number.">Remove the 9% result so readers see only the larger number.</button></div></div>
    </div>
    <div class="drill-actions"><button class="lesson-btn" data-check-quiz>Check</button><button class="lesson-btn" data-reset-quiz>Reset</button></div><p class="feedback" data-feedback></p>
  </div>

  <h2>Build careful attribution and a scope-correct summary</h2>
  <div class="lead-rule"></div>
  <div class="tile-game" data-tile-builder data-answer="according to the city memo total food waste fell by nine percent" data-hint="Begin with According to the city memo, then state the total-site result and its percentage." data-fix="According to the city memo, total food waste fell by nine percent." data-success-message="Correct. According to introduces the source, and the summary reports the total-site percentage without replacing it with the category result.">
    <h3>Build 1: attribute the total-site result</h3><p class="tile-target">Name the source and preserve the 9% total-food-waste figure.</p>
    <div class="tile-bank" data-build-bank><button class="tile" data-build-tile="percent">percent</button><button class="tile" data-build-tile="memo">memo</button><button class="tile" data-build-tile="nine">nine</button><button class="tile" data-build-tile="city">city</button><button class="tile" data-build-tile="waste">waste</button><button class="tile" data-build-tile="to">to</button><button class="tile" data-build-tile="fell">fell</button><button class="tile" data-build-tile="the">the</button><button class="tile" data-build-tile="total">total</button><button class="tile" data-build-tile="according">According</button><button class="tile" data-build-tile="food">food</button><button class="tile" data-build-tile="by">by</button></div>
    <div class="build-area" data-build-area data-placeholder="Tap words in order"></div><div class="drill-actions"><button class="lesson-btn" data-check-build>Check</button><button class="lesson-btn" data-reset-build>Reset</button></div><p class="feedback" data-feedback></p>
  </div>
  <div class="tile-game" data-tile-builder data-answer="the one-month pilot suggests a promising result but needs broader testing" data-hint="Begin with The one-month pilot suggests, then add a promising result but needs broader testing." data-fix="The one-month pilot suggests a promising result but needs broader testing." data-success-message="Correct. Suggests calibrates the interpretation, and the final clause preserves the need for broader evidence.">
    <h3>Build 2: report promise without claiming proof</h3><p class="tile-target">Keep the pilot timeframe and the evidence limitation.</p>
    <div class="tile-bank" data-build-bank><button class="tile" data-build-tile="broader">broader</button><button class="tile" data-build-tile="result">result</button><button class="tile" data-build-tile="one-month">one-month</button><button class="tile" data-build-tile="but">but</button><button class="tile" data-build-tile="testing">testing</button><button class="tile" data-build-tile="a">a</button><button class="tile" data-build-tile="the">The</button><button class="tile" data-build-tile="promising">promising</button><button class="tile" data-build-tile="pilot">pilot</button><button class="tile" data-build-tile="needs">needs</button><button class="tile" data-build-tile="suggests">suggests</button></div>
    <div class="build-area" data-build-area data-placeholder="Tap words in order"></div><div class="drill-actions"><button class="lesson-btn" data-check-build>Check</button><button class="lesson-btn" data-reset-build>Reset</button></div><p class="feedback" data-feedback></p>
